::GEO_U04_L01_Q01::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L01_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>A logo is enlarged from a width of 4 cm to 10 cm. What is the scale factor from the original to the image? {
=A. 2.5#Scale factor compares image length to original length: 10 divided by 4 is 2.5.
~B. 6#This subtracts the lengths instead of comparing them multiplicatively.
~C. 14#This adds the lengths and does not describe scale factor.
~D. 0.4#This is the reverse scale factor from image to original.
}

::GEO_U04_L01_Q02::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L01_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>Two similar rectangles have corresponding heights 7 and 21. What is the scale factor from the smaller rectangle to the larger rectangle? {
~A. 14#This subtracts the heights instead of using a multiplier.
=B. 3#The scale factor is 21 divided by 7, which equals 3.
~C. 28#This adds the heights and does not represent similarity scaling.
~D. &frac13;#This is the scale factor from larger to smaller, not smaller to larger.
}

::GEO_U04_L01_Q03::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L01_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>A scale copy changes a side from 9 units to 6 units. What is the scale factor from the original to the copy? {
~A. 3#This uses a whole-number multiplier that would enlarge the side.
~B. 15#This adds the side lengths and does not describe scale factor.
=C. <sup>2</sup>&frasl;<sub>3</sub>#The copy-to-original ratio is 6 divided by 9, which simplifies to <sup>2</sup>&frasl;<sub>3</sub>.
~D. <sup>3</sup>&frasl;<sub>2</sub>#This is the reverse scale factor from the copy back to the original.
}

::GEO_U04_L01_Q04::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L01_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>Figure A has side lengths 5, 8, and 11. Figure B has corresponding side lengths 10, 16, and 22. What evidence supports similarity? {
~A. Only one side is doubled.#All three corresponding sides are doubled, not just one.
~B. The figures are similar because they are drawn near each other.#Location in a diagram is not evidence of similarity.
~C. The side differences are all equal.#The differences are not all equal, and similarity depends on ratios rather than differences.
=D. All corresponding side ratios are 2.#Each side in Figure B is twice the matching side in Figure A, so the side ratios are equal.
}

::GEO_U04_L01_Q05::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L01_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>Figure A has side lengths 3, 4, and 6. Figure B has corresponding side lengths 6, 8, and 12. What is the common scale factor? {
=A. 2#Each corresponding side in Figure B is 2 times the side in Figure A.
~B. 3#A scale factor of 3 would make the sides 9, 12, and 18.
~C. 4#A scale factor of 4 would make the sides 12, 16, and 24.
~D. 1#A scale factor of 1 would mean the corresponding lengths are unchanged.
}

::GEO_U04_L01_Q06::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L01_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>A drawing uses a scale factor of 4 from a small triangle to a large triangle. A 6-inch side on the small triangle corresponds to what length on the large triangle? {
~A. 10 inches#Adding 4 treats the scale factor as an amount to add instead of a multiplier.
=B. 24 inches#Multiply the original side by the scale factor: 6 times 4 equals 24.
~C. 2 inches#Dividing by 4 scales in the opposite direction.
~D. 18 inches#Multiplying by 3 does not use the given scale factor of 4.
}

::GEO_U04_L01_Q07::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L01_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>A scale factor of &frac12; is applied to a figure. What happens to each corresponding side length? {
~A. Each side length doubles.#Doubling would use a scale factor of 2.
~B. Each side length increases by &frac12; unit.#Scale factors multiply lengths; they do not add a fixed amount.
=C. Each side length is halved.#A scale factor of &frac12; multiplies every length by one-half.
~D. Only one side length changes.#A dilation scale factor applies to all corresponding lengths.
}

::GEO_U04_L01_Q08::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L01_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>Which statement gives mathematical evidence that two figures are similar? {
~A. The figures appear to have the same shape.#Appearance alone is not mathematical evidence.
~B. One pair of sides has the same length.#One side pair alone does not establish similarity.
~C. The figures are both shaded.#Shading is visual decoration, not similarity evidence.
=D. Corresponding angles match and corresponding side ratios are equal.#Similarity requires equal corresponding angles and proportional corresponding side lengths.
}

::GEO_U04_L01_Q09::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L01_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>Two figures have corresponding side ratios 4:6, 6:9, and 8:12. What do these ratios show? {
=A. The corresponding side lengths are proportional.#Each ratio simplifies to 2:3, so the side lengths are proportional.
~B. The figures are congruent.#Congruent figures would have a scale factor of 1; these ratios show a different common ratio.
~C. Only the first two sides are proportional.#All three listed ratios simplify to the same relationship.
~D. No comparison is possible.#The ratios provide enough information to compare corresponding sides.
}

::GEO_U04_L01_Q10::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L01_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>A scale factor from Figure P to Figure Q is 1. What does that mean about corresponding side lengths? {
~A. They are doubled.#Doubling would require a scale factor of 2.
=B. They are equal in length.#A scale factor of 1 leaves every corresponding side length unchanged.
~C. They are halved.#Halving would require a scale factor of &frac12;.
~D. They cannot be compared.#A scale factor gives a direct comparison of corresponding lengths.
}

::GEO_U04_L01_Q11::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L01_Q11<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>A rectangle has sides 6 and 10. A similar rectangle has a side corresponding to 6 that measures 18. What side corresponds to 10? {
~A. 22#Adding 12 repeats the first side difference, but similarity uses multiplication.
~B. 15#This uses a scale factor of 1.5 instead of 3.
=C. 30#The scale factor is 18 divided by 6, or 3; therefore 10 times 3 equals 30.
~D. 28#This does not use the matching scale factor from corresponding sides.
}

::GEO_U04_L01_Q12::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L01_Q12<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>A model uses scale factor 5 from a drawing to the actual object. A drawing length is 3 cm. What is the actual length? {
~A. 8 cm#Adding the scale factor does not apply similarity scaling.
~B. 2 cm#Subtracting does not apply a scale factor.
~C. 1.67 cm#Dividing by 5 would scale from actual object back to drawing.
=D. 15 cm#Multiply the drawing length by the scale factor: 3 times 5 equals 15.
}

::GEO_U04_L01_Q13::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L01_Q13<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>A triangle has sides 4, 7, and 9. A scale copy has sides 12, 21, and 27. Are the triangles similar by side ratios? {
=A. Yes, each corresponding side ratio is 3.#The second triangle has sides 3 times the first triangle, so the side ratios are equal.
~B. No, because the side differences are not equal.#Similarity depends on ratios, not equal differences.
~C. No, because the triangles have different sizes.#Similar figures may have different sizes when side lengths are proportional.
~D. Yes, because only one side ratio is 3.#All corresponding side ratios must be checked, not just one.
}

::GEO_U04_L01_Q14::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L01_Q14<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>Which pair of ratios is proportional? {
~A. <sup>3</sup>&frasl;<sub>5</sub> and <sup>5</sup>&frasl;<sub>3</sub>#These are reciprocals, not equal ratios.
=B. <sup>8</sup>&frasl;<sub>12</sub> and <sup>2</sup>&frasl;<sub>3</sub>#<sup>8</sup>&frasl;<sub>12</sub> simplifies to <sup>2</sup>&frasl;<sub>3</sub>, so the ratios are equal.
~C. <sup>4</sup>&frasl;<sub>9</sub> and <sup>8</sup>&frasl;<sub>12</sub>#These simplify to different values.
~D. <sup>7</sup>&frasl;<sub>10</sub> and <sup>10</sup>&frasl;<sub>7</sub>#These are reciprocals, not equal ratios.
}

::GEO_U04_L01_Q15::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L01_Q15<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>A poster is reduced with scale factor 0.25. Which statement is true? {
~A. Each corresponding length becomes four times as long.#That would use a scale factor of 4.
~B. Only the width changes.#A scale factor affects every corresponding length.
=C. Each corresponding length becomes one-fourth as long.#A scale factor of 0.25 is equivalent to one-fourth.
~D. Lengths increase by 0.25 unit.#Scale factors multiply lengths rather than adding a fixed amount.
}

::GEO_U04_L01_Q16::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L01_Q16<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>Two figures have corresponding sides 8 and 20. What is the scale factor from the first figure to the second? {
~A. 12#This subtracts the side lengths instead of comparing them by ratio.
~B. 28#This adds side lengths and does not give a scale factor.
~C. 0.4#This is the reverse scale factor from the second figure to the first.
=D. 2.5#The scale factor is 20 divided by 8, which equals 2.5.
}

::GEO_U04_L01_Q17::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L01_Q17<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>A similar figure has a side length 45 corresponding to original side length 15. Which scale factor was used? {
=A. 3#Divide the image length by the original length: 45 divided by 15 equals 3.
~B. 30#This subtracts the lengths instead of using a ratio.
~C. 60#This adds the lengths and does not describe similarity.
~D. &frac13;#This is the reverse scale factor.
}

::GEO_U04_L01_Q18::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L01_Q18<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>A scale factor of 2.2 is applied to a segment of length 5. What is the image length? {
~A. 7.2#Adding 2.2 treats the scale factor as an addend.
=B. 11#Multiply 5 by 2.2 to get 11.
~C. 2.8#Subtracting the scale factor does not describe dilation.
~D. 2.27#Dividing by 2.2 scales in the opposite direction.
}

::GEO_U04_L01_Q19::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L01_Q19<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>A student claims two quadrilaterals are similar because both have four sides. What is missing? {
~A. The color of each figure#Color does not determine similarity.
~B. The area of the page#The page area is unrelated to figure similarity.
=C. Evidence about corresponding angles and side ratios#Having four sides only identifies both as quadrilaterals; similarity requires angle and proportional side evidence.
~D. The names of the vertices only#Vertex names help with correspondence but do not alone prove similarity.
}

::GEO_U04_L01_Q20::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L01_Q20<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>Side ratios from Figure A to Figure B are 2, 2, 2, and 2. What conclusion is reasonable if corresponding angles also match? {
~A. The figures cannot be compared.#The ratios and angle evidence allow a comparison.
~B. The figures must have scale factor 1.#The scale factor shown by the ratios is 2, not 1.
~C. The figures are unrelated because they have four sides.#Four-sided figures can be similar when correspondence evidence is present.
=D. The figures are similar.#Equal corresponding side ratios together with matching angles support similarity.
}

::GEO_U04_L01_Q21::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L01_Q21<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>A small pentagon is dilated to a larger pentagon with scale factor 4. A side of the small pentagon is 2.5 units. What is the corresponding larger side? {
=A. 10 units#Multiply 2.5 by 4 to get 10 units.
~B. 6.5 units#Adding 4 does not use the scale factor as a multiplier.
~C. 1.6 units#Dividing by 4 scales in the reverse direction.
~D. 8 units#This does not use the given side length 2.5 correctly.
}

::GEO_U04_L01_Q22::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L01_Q22<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>If corresponding side lengths are proportional, what must still be checked for polygon similarity? {
~A. The drawing color#Color is not part of similarity.
=B. Corresponding angles#For polygons, corresponding angles must also match.
~C. The page orientation#Page orientation does not determine similarity.
~D. Whether the names use the same letters#Letter names help with correspondence but do not establish similarity.
}

::GEO_U04_L01_Q23::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L01_Q23<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>A figure is enlarged by scale factor 6. What happens to a 1.5-unit side? {
~A. It becomes 7.5 units.#Adding 6 does not use scale factor correctly.
~B. It becomes 4.5 units.#Multiplying by 3 does not use the given scale factor.
=C. It becomes 9 units.#Multiply 1.5 by 6 to get 9.
~D. It becomes 0.25 unit.#Dividing by 6 scales in the opposite direction.
}

::GEO_U04_L01_Q24::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L01_Q24<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>Two diagrams look alike but have no measurements or markings. What can be concluded? {
~A. They are definitely similar.#Appearance alone is not proof of similarity.
~B. They are definitely congruent.#Congruence also requires evidence, not appearance alone.
~C. Their scale factor is 1.#A scale factor cannot be determined without corresponding measurements or other evidence.
=D. No similarity conclusion is justified from appearance alone.#Similarity needs evidence such as side ratios, angle measures, or a transformation.
}

::GEO_U04_L01_Q25::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U04_L01_Q25<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>Readiness Support Only<br><br>Question:<br>A rectangular photo is resized from 8 inches wide to 12 inches wide. What scale factor was used from the original photo to the resized photo? {
=A. 1.5#The scale factor is image width divided by original width: 12 divided by 8 equals 1.5.
~B. 4#This subtracts the widths instead of comparing them with a ratio.
~C. 20#This adds the widths and does not describe multiplicative scaling.
~D. <sup>2</sup>&frasl;<sub>3</sub>#This is the reverse scale factor from the resized photo back to the original.
}
